\section{Methods and Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

\subsection{Architectures and Benchmark}
\label{sec:setup_overview}

Seven VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families provide the testbed for our pruning study (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}).

\textbf{Linear projection.} $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens) maps SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} patches through a single linear layer into a PaliGemma backbone and generates actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. $\pi_0.5$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B parameters, 768 vision tokens from three cameras: left wrist, right wrist, base) applies a linear projection with learned $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling into the same PaliGemma backbone with flow matching action generation; the larger token count arises from processing three camera views rather than one. X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) augments its linear projection with a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}; self-attention in these layers introduces cross-token mixing that makes the effective pipeline more complex than the nominal ``linear projection'' label suggests. All three models share the linear projection label, yet their post-projection processing spans a spectrum from no additional computation ($\pi_0$) to a full transformer encoder (X-VLA).

\textbf{MLP projector.} OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters, 256 tokens) fuses DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders through a three-layer MLP (GELU activations) that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone. OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} shares this MLP connector with an optimized fine-tuning action head; we include OFT for attention entropy comparison only, as both metrics match OpenVLA and confirm the connector determines token importance structure. CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact} uses an MLP projector and retains 74.2\% SR under 78\% pruning as reported by EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla}; we cite this result as literature corroboration of the graded response pattern rather than from our own evaluation.

\textbf{Spatial folding.} SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, 128 tokens from two cameras) encodes each camera image with SigLIP into 1024 patches, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4{\times}4$ block into one token by channel concatenation (64 per camera). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4{\times}4$ receptive field. The tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\cite{yang2024qwen2} with flow matching action generation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

We evaluate on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks organized into three suites that test complementary skills: Object (10 tasks requiring correct object identification), Spatial (10 tasks requiring spatial reasoning about relative positions), and Goal (10 tasks requiring goal-state recognition). Each task uses 50 evaluation episodes (500 total per suite). Object serves as the primary evaluation; Spatial and Goal provide cross-suite validation of findings. Closed-loop success rate (SR) with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals is the primary metric for single-seed results; multi-seed evaluations report mean $\pm$ standard deviation. Full fine-tuning baselines on Object: SmolVLA 44.8\%, OpenVLA 74.0\%, X-VLA 99.2\%, $\pi_0.5$ 98.2\%.

\subsection{Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:pruning_protocol}

Two complementary pruning methods bracket each model's robustness from above and below. Both methods physically remove unselected tokens from the sequence (reducing the actual input length to the LLM), rather than zeroing them out; the distinction matters because zero-masking preserves positional structure while physical removal does not.

\textbf{L2 norm-based removal} (importance scoring) ranks vision tokens by their L2 norm and physically removes the lowest-norm fraction. Norm ranking preserves the most informative tokens by construction, so L2 removal provides a more favorable test of pruning tolerance. The underlying assumption is that higher-norm tokens carry more task-relevant information; Section~\ref{sec:scaling_law} examines when this assumption holds and when it fails.

\textbf{Random removal} (uniform selection) selects tokens uniformly at random and physically removes the unselected ones. Random removal provides a conservative lower bound because it makes no attempt to preserve high-importance tokens. For models with near-uniform token norms, however, random selection can outperform L2 by preserving spatial coverage rather than clustering on correlated patches.

Model-specific retention ratios reflect each architecture's token count and expected sensitivity. $\pi_0$ is evaluated offline only ($k{=}0.1$, normalized L1 action loss) because its deployment environment precludes closed-loop testing. $\pi_0.5$ uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7\}$ under both L2 and random removal in closed-loop evaluation; the dual-method design enables direct comparison within a single architecture. X-VLA spans $k \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with L2 removal across three seeds; this is the finest-grained retention sweep in our study and provides sufficient resolution to locate sharp onset thresholds. OpenVLA uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with random removal as the primary protocol and L2 for direct comparison. SmolVLA tests seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio ($k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}).

The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA pair forms a controlled natural experiment: both consume 256 SigLIP tokens from the same encoder lineage, differing only in their connector (linear projection vs.\ three-layer MLP). Any performance gap at matched retention ratios isolates the connector as the causal factor rather than encoder capacity or token count. Comparing OpenVLA (random removal, harder test) against X-VLA (L2 removal, easier test) further strengthens architecture-dependent conclusions, because any observed fragility in X-VLA cannot be attributed to an unfavorable scoring method.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

A cross-attention pooling module (22.2M parameters, $\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) is inserted between the pixel-shuffle connector and the LLM backbone. $M$ learned queries attend over all 128 source tokens via standard scaled dot-product attention, producing $M$ compressed representations that replace the original 128 tokens as input to the LLM. The compression sweep covers $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$; $M{=}16$ represents 87.5\% token reduction while $M{=}128$ serves as a passthrough control that adds parameters without reducing tokens.

A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) serves as an architecture control, testing whether the benefit comes from cross-attention specifically or from re-encoding in general. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\% SR, statistically indistinguishable from the 44.8\% baseline ($p > 0.9$); any gain from VTR is therefore architectural, not from additional optimization steps. VTR results are evaluated with three seeds per configuration to ensure reproducibility. Training details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:diagnostic_metrics}

Two metrics computed from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames characterize each model's vision token structure at zero additional cost.

\textbf{Token importance CV} is the coefficient of variation of L2 norms across all vision tokens. High CV signals a heterogeneous importance distribution where some tokens carry substantially more information than others; low CV indicates near-uniform norms. CV tracks pruning tolerance continuously across the seven-model taxonomy (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy_overview}): uniformly critical models cluster at CV $< 0.06$, threshold-graded at $\sim$0.15, smooth graded at $\sim$0.22, and catastrophic cliff at $> 0.3$.

\textbf{Attention entropy ratio} is the normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights over vision tokens, computed from the final LLM layer's cross-attention distribution. Low entropy indicates concentrated attention on a few tokens, while high entropy reflects diffuse attention across the full token set. CV measures the input-side importance gradient; entropy captures how the downstream LLM allocates attention and provides a complementary view of the same structural property. OFT and OpenVLA share nearly identical entropy values (0.667 vs.\ 0.661); the match confirms that the connector, not the action head, determines token utilization patterns. Section~\ref{sec:scaling_law} demonstrates that CV also predicts the optimal pruning strategy: low-CV models require coverage-preserving methods (random selection), while higher-CV models can benefit from importance-based scoring.

